# CeraLive prune-paths manifest — the SINGLE SOURCE for every "safe to strip
# after packages are configured" glob in the image pipeline (Todo 29,
# update-system-overhaul). One glob pattern per line; blank lines and lines
# starting with `#` are ignored by every reader.
#
# THREE CONSUMERS, ONE LIST:
#
#   1. Build-time: lib/stages/mkosi.sh generates
#      mkosi/mkosi.images/runtime/mkosi.local.conf (gitignored, regenerated on
#      every real build) with `RemoveFiles=<these globs, comma-joined>`. mkosi
#      APPENDS list-type settings across config files (its own documented
#      cascade rule — a repeated setting is a collection merge, not an
#      override), so this ADDS to, and never replaces, the runtime layer's
#      existing static RemoveFiles= line (arch-specific Mesa/LLVM globs stay
#      there, hand-maintained — see mkosi.images/runtime/mkosi.conf for why).
#
#      SCOPE, DELIBERATELY NARROW: only content that is safe to remove at ANY
#      layer, including base/platform/runtime, belongs here — i.e. content
#      that is NOT update-alternatives-registered. `tests/mkosi-contract.bats`
#      "the deferred prune runs at the final app layer only" enforces this by
#      refusing any `/usr/share/(doc|man)` reference in base/platform/runtime.
#      Do NOT add doc/man/X11/icon paths here; those stay the app layer's own
#      prune_final_image_payload()/prune_package_docs() hardcoded set, run
#      strictly after every layer's packages (including first-party apps) have
#      finished configuring.
#
#   2. Device-installed copy: the runtime layer's chroot postinst decodes the
#      base64-forwarded content of THIS file (CERALIVE_PRUNE_PATHS_B64,
#      PassEnvironment=, since a subimage chroot cannot read a path above
#      $SRCDIR — see mkosi/mkosi.conf's own comment on target-release.env for
#      the identical constraint) and writes it verbatim to
#      /usr/lib/ceralive/prune-paths.list. Two on-device readers:
#        - /usr/libexec/ceralive/ceralive-reprune (mkosi/runtime/ceralive-reprune.sh),
#          a DPkg::Post-Invoke hook (/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/80ceralive-reprune)
#          that re-applies these globs after EVERY future apt transaction, so
#          a package that reintroduces a pruned path (e.g. locale files
#          shipped by a newly upgraded dependency) does not silently undo the
#          build-time prune. Never fails the apt transaction (logs, exit 0).
#        - ceralive-slot-sync's dpkg --verify comparison (todo 26), which
#          filters expected cross-slot differences through this same file.
#
#   3. The app layer's prune_package_docs() (mkosi.images/app/mkosi.postinst.chroot)
#      ALSO applies these same globs (reading the already-installed device copy
#      at /usr/lib/ceralive/prune-paths.list, inherited via BaseTrees=%O/runtime)
#      as an additional pass alongside its existing /usr/share/doc
#      copyright-only reduction. Safe and redundant-by-design at that point —
#      the runtime layer already stripped these — proving the same manifest
#      drives every layer without duplicating the literal glob list.
#
# CONTENT: the locale/i18n strip, safe everywhere (not alternatives-registered).
/usr/share/locale/*
/usr/lib/locale/locale-archive
